The investment thesis for Micron Technology (MU) centers on its role as a primary "toll-gate" provider for the AI infrastructure layer. While the semiconductor industry is historically cyclical, the transition toward High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM3E/HBM4) represents a structural shift in the global computing stack. Micron’s aggressive capital deployment—evidenced by a 90.5% CapEx/OCF ratio—serves as a formidable barrier to entry, creating a high-fixed-cost structure that facilitates significant operational leverage. As production scales to meet the "profitability imperative" of cloud and edge inference, the expansion of operating margins from 5.20% toward a projected 26.20% provides a compelling path for valuation re-rating.

However, the investment must be weighed against a complex macroeconomic backdrop. A 10-Year Treasury Yield of 4.47% and the Federal Reserve’s shift away from forward guidance introduce a "volatility premium" into the discount rate. While the aggressive thesis highlights a PEG of 0.31 as a sign of market underestimation, the conservative analysis correctly identifies the risk of "pricing floor" friction in take-or-pay contracts. To mitigate these risks, the final verdict adopts a disciplined valuation that acknowledges the high-intensity investment phase while capturing the asymmetric upside of the AI-native computing transition.

The "CapEx Moat" is the defining characteristic of this position. By securing supply chain dominance now, Micron positions itself to capture the massive volume of inference requests that will eventually dwarf training clusters. The risk of a "liquidity breach" is moderated by multi-year procurement contracts with hyperscalers, providing a buffer against short-term demand volatility. We conclude that MU is a high-conviction infrastructure play, justified by its dominance in the most critical hardware bottleneck of the decade.

**Final Executive Decision:**

